<h2 id="examples-section-nonsplit-locally-split" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="0H7F">110.14</span> Nonsplit locally split sequence</h2>

<p>
Consider the short exact sequence 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to M \to \bigoplus \nolimits _{p\text{ prime}} \mathbf{Z}_{(p)} \to \mathbf{Q} \to 0  \]
</div>
<p>
 where $M$ is the kernel of the map on the right which uses the inclusion $\mathbf{Z}_{(p)} \to \mathbf{Q}$ on each summand. This sequence of $\mathbf{Z}$-modules is nonsplit because there are no nonzero homomorphisms $\mathbf{Q} \to \mathbf{Z}_{(p)}$. On the other hand, if we localize at any prime $p$, then the sequence becomes split. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="0H7G">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0H7G">Lemma <span data-tag="0H7G">110.14.1</span>.</a> There exists a ring $R$ and a nonsplit sequence of modules which becomes split Zariski locally. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      See discussion above. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
